  design in now and painful to retrofit, because retrofitting means finding every mutation path after the fact and
  hoping you caught them all. This sub-project ships no notifications.

  [1mB — Relevance.[0m What makes a task one you "care about": you created it, you're assigned, you commented, you explicitly
  watched it. Plus the inverse — muting. This is where these systems usually fail: the default ends up being "tell
  everyone about everything," people learn to ignore the badge, and the feature is dead even though every line of it
  works. It's also the most expensive thing to change later, since the rule leaks into every query and every screen.

  [1mC — Notifications proper.[0m Turn change-log entries into per-user notifications, with unread state and an in-app inbox.
  This is the thing you actually asked for, and it's small once A and B exist.

  [1mD — Out-of-app delivery.[0m Email, push, digests, per-user preferences, quiet hours. Deliberately last. Swapping delivery
  channels later is a contained change, and you can't tune digest batching sensibly until you've watched real event
  volume from C.

  My recommendation is A → B → C, each with its own spec and plan, with D deferred until you've used C for a bit. A is
  unglamorous, but there is no honest way to build C without it.

  Two things I want to check before I keep going.
